\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Where the effect enters, and what it moves least.} (\textbf  {a}) Cooperation by notation regime across the game. Solid lines are the three models that met ten scales, dashed the three that met three; the four positions are round one and three equal blocks of the remaining rounds. On the three-scale grid the gap between the regimes is open at round one, before any history exists, and stays open. On the ten-scale grid the three regimes sit within two points of each other at every position, which is figure~\ref {fig:notation}a read round by round. (\textbf  {b}) Opening-move cooperation against the payoff scale, one line per model, colours as in figure~\ref {fig:response}. (\textbf  {c}) The largest shift across scales for six measures of the same games, against the same permutation null. Dark bars are the ten-scale models, pale the three-scale ones; grey marks a measure whose shift does not clear its null. Cooperation and the opening move shift in both groups; the rate at which the two agents fail to match is the smallest mover in both, and the only one of the six that fails its null anywhere.}}{13}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:where}{{4}{13}{\textbf {Where the effect enters, and what it moves least.} (\textbf {a}) Cooperation by notation regime across the game. Solid lines are the three models that met ten scales, dashed the three that met three; the four positions are round one and three equal blocks of the remaining rounds. On the three-scale grid the gap between the regimes is open at round one, before any history exists, and stays open. On the ten-scale grid the three regimes sit within two points of each other at every position, which is figure~\ref {fig:notation}a read round by round. (\textbf {b}) Opening-move cooperation against the payoff scale, one line per model, colours as in figure~\ref {fig:response}. (\textbf {c}) The largest shift across scales for six measures of the same games, against the same permutation null. Dark bars are the ten-scale models, pale the three-scale ones; grey marks a measure whose shift does not clear its null. Cooperation and the opening move shift in both groups; the rate at which the two agents fail to match is the smallest mover in both, and the only one of the six that fails its null anywhere}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.7}What the rescaling moves least}{13}{subsection.3.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:invariant}{{3.7}{13}{What the rescaling moves least}{subsection.3.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {What the payoff scale carries with it.} (\textbf  {a}) The effect of being told to be cooperative rather than selfish, scale by scale, \emph  {one line per model}; colours as in figure~\ref {fig:response}, solid the three models that met ten scales and dashed the three that met three. Every model moves upward across the fractional-to-unit boundary, but only three of the six cross zero: the sign of the effect is a property of the model. The average of these lines crosses zero although half of them never do, which is why no pooled curve is drawn. (\textbf  {b}) Cooperation by prompt language across the sweep, ten-scale models. The five languages do not move together. (\textbf  {c}) The share of agent-games carrying each strategy label, against the payoff scale, with the two groups of models encoded as in figure~\ref {fig:where}a. The two unconditional rules carry most of the movement in both, and much more of it on the coarser grid.}}{14}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gating}{{5}{14}{\textbf {What the payoff scale carries with it.} (\textbf {a}) The effect of being told to be cooperative rather than selfish, scale by scale, \emph {one line per model}; colours as in figure~\ref {fig:response}, solid the three models that met ten scales and dashed the three that met three. Every model moves upward across the fractional-to-unit boundary, but only three of the six cross zero: the sign of the effect is a property of the model. The average of these lines crosses zero although half of them never do, which is why no pooled curve is drawn. (\textbf {b}) Cooperation by prompt language across the sweep, ten-scale models. The five languages do not move together. (\textbf {c}) The share of agent-games carrying each strategy label, against the payoff scale, with the two groups of models encoded as in figure~\ref {fig:where}a. The two unconditional rules carry most of the movement in both, and much more of it on the coarser grid}{figure.caption.5}{}}
